AHS-002 — Architectural Principles

Document ID: AHS-002
Title: Architectural Principles
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the canonical architectural principles governing the design, implementation, evolution, and operation of the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

These principles establish the non-negotiable rules that ensure architectural consistency, interoperability, scalability, and long-term maintainability across all Atlas-compliant systems.

2. Scope

These principles apply to:

All Atlas specifications
All Atlas components
All Atlas services
All cognitive workflows
All interfaces
All implementations

Every Atlas implementation SHALL comply with these principles unless explicitly exempted by a future normative specification.

3. Principle 1 — Conceptual Integrity

The architecture SHALL preserve the canonical concepts defined by ACC without modification or duplication.

Each architectural component SHALL reference, rather than redefine, Atlas Core Concepts.

Rationale: A single source of truth prevents semantic drift and ensures consistency across the ecosystem.

4. Principle 2 — Separation of Concerns

Each architectural component SHALL have a single, clearly defined responsibility.

Responsibilities SHALL NOT overlap unless explicitly specified.

For example:

Evidence components validate information.
Memory components preserve understanding.
Reasoning components derive conclusions.
Decision Support components communicate recommendations.

Rationale: Clear boundaries improve maintainability and reduce coupling.

5. Principle 3 — Modularity

The architecture SHALL consist of independently composable modules.

Each module SHALL:

expose defined interfaces,
minimize dependencies,
support independent evolution where practical.

Rationale: Modularity enables flexibility, reuse, and scalable development.

6. Principle 4 — Explainability by Design

Every engineering conclusion produced by Atlas SHALL be explainable through its supporting evidence, reasoning, context, assumptions, and constraints.

Explainability SHALL be an inherent architectural capability, not an optional feature.

Rationale: Engineering decisions require transparency and justification.

7. Principle 5 — Traceability by Design

Every significant cognitive artifact SHALL be traceable to its origin.

Traceability SHALL enable reconstruction of the complete cognitive pathway from Engineering Reality to Decision Support.

Rationale: Provenance is essential for verification, auditing, and continuous improvement.

8. Principle 6 — Evidence-Driven Cognition

Engineering Understanding, Knowledge, Reasoning, and Capability SHALL be grounded in Engineering Evidence as defined by ACC.

Architectural components SHALL avoid unsupported conclusions.

Rationale: Reliable cognition depends on verifiable evidence.

9. Principle 7 — Human-Centered Decision Support

Atlas SHALL augment engineering decision-making rather than replace engineering judgment.

Decision authority SHALL remain with appropriately authorized human stakeholders unless explicitly delegated by system policy.

Rationale: Atlas is designed as a decision support framework, not an autonomous decision-maker.

10. Principle 8 — Technology Independence

The architecture SHALL remain independent of:

Programming languages
AI models
Databases
Message brokers
Cloud providers
Operating systems
Deployment environments

Technology choices SHALL implement the architecture without altering its semantics.

Rationale: Longevity and portability require implementation independence.

11. Principle 9 — Extensibility

The architecture SHALL support the introduction of new engineering domains, services, and capabilities without modifying existing canonical concepts.

Extensions SHALL remain compatible with ACC and AHS.

Rationale: Atlas is intended to evolve across engineering disciplines.

12. Principle 10 — Interoperability

Architectural components SHALL communicate through well-defined interfaces and shared semantics.

Implementations SHALL avoid proprietary interactions that compromise portability or conformance.

Rationale: Interoperability enables integration across tools, organizations, and platforms.

13. Principle 11 — Scalability

The architecture SHALL support growth in:

Data volume
Knowledge volume
Cognitive complexity
Number of users
Number of agents
Number of engineering domains

Scalability SHALL preserve correctness and architectural integrity.

Rationale: Atlas is designed for systems ranging from individual engineers to enterprise-scale platforms.

14. Principle 12 — Evolution with Stability

The architecture SHALL evolve through versioned, backward-compatible changes whenever practical.

Breaking changes SHALL require a major version revision and explicit migration guidance.

Rationale: Controlled evolution protects long-term compatibility and ecosystem stability.

15. Conformance

An implementation conforms to this specification if it:

Adheres to all architectural principles.
Preserves the canonical semantics defined by ACC.
Maintains architectural consistency.
Documents any approved deviations.
16. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce additional architectural principles, provided they:

Do not contradict existing principles.
Remain consistent with ACC.
Preserve interoperability and conceptual integrity.